course: money
lesson: 33
title: Adverts and how they work on you
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An advert is a message paid for by someone who wants you to buy something, and it is made very carefully to make you want it.
explain: Picture a TV ad with kids laughing, music pumping and a toy zooming round, then "Only £29.99!": Freya shouts "I NEED that", but that is the ad talking, because nearly £30 is six weeks of £5 pocket money.
explain: Ads sell feelings: happy, cool, grown-up; they use famous footballers, cartoon animals, free toys, camera tricks that make a 10cm toy look 50cm tall, and numbers like "9 out of 10 kids love it" without saying which kids.
explain: Free apps and websites earn money from ads, so your attention is what they sell; that is why ads pop up every few minutes and why trainers follow you around after one search.
explain: The trick to beat an ad is to ask questions: who paid for this, what do they want me to feel, and says who?
fact: An advert is a message trying to sell something; a target audience is the group of people it is aimed at.
fact: £29.99 is just 1p under £30, which is 6 weeks of £5 pocket money.
fact: An endorsement is when a famous person promotes a product, often paid a lot, maybe £1 million; it does not prove they really use it.
fact: A survey asks people questions; "9 out of 10" might mean only 10 people were asked, so small or chosen groups can mislead.
fact: Free apps are ad-supported: one ad every 3 minutes over 30 minutes is 30 ÷ 3 = 10 ads.
fact: Misleading means giving the wrong idea; an ad can make a 10cm toy look 50cm tall, which is 5 times bigger than real.
fact: An affiliate is paid for each sale they bring; a £20 product with 10% off costs £18, and the YouTuber might get £2 for the sale.
fact: Targeted ads are chosen for you from what you look at online; cookies are small files that help websites track you.
fact: Persuasion is trying to change what someone thinks or does; asking "what do they want me to feel?" helps you stay in control.
fact: Marketing is everything a company does to sell; cereal with a toy at £3.50 instead of plain cereal at £1.50 means paying £2 for a tiny toy.
fact: Some countries limit adverts for unhealthy food aimed at children.
fact: A slogan is a short catchy phrase; "the best pizza in town" is an opinion, not a checked fact.
exercise: What is an advert? | A paid message made to make you want to buy something | A news report that has been checked carefully by independent experts | A school lesson about how toys are made in big factories abroad | A list of the cheapest prices in all the shops in town today
exercise: The toy costs £29.99. Freya gets £5 pocket money a week. About how many weeks is that? | 6 weeks | 3 weeks | 30 weeks | 5 weeks
exercise: Why do ads show kids laughing with the toy? | So you link the toy with feeling happy | Because the kids were told a really funny joke just before filming | Because the law says every advert for children must be funny | To show that the toy can tell jokes
exercise: Jake's favourite footballer drinks a fizzy drink in an ad. What is likely true? | He was paid, maybe a lot, to appear in the ad | He drinks it every single day before every match he plays | Footballers must drink it by the rules of football | It makes footballers play better
exercise: What is an endorsement? | A famous person promoting a product, often for money | A survey of ten children asked what they think about a product | A law limiting ads for unhealthy food on children's TV | A refund for a product you didn't like
exercise: An ad says "9 out of 10 kids love it!" Isla asks, "Which kids?" Why is that a good question? | It might be only 10 chosen kids, which can mislead | It is always every single child in the whole country, so it's fair | Kids are never asked anything in surveys | Numbers in ads are always checked by the police
exercise: An ad pops up every 3 minutes in a 30-minute game. How many ads is that? | 10 | 3 | 33 | 90
exercise: How does a free game app make money? | By showing you ads and selling your attention | By secretly charging your parents a little bit every single week | By getting paid by your school for every child who plays it | It doesn't make money, because it is run as a charity for kids
exercise: The toy looks 50cm tall in the ad but is 10cm in the shop. How many times bigger did it look? | 5 times | 40 times | 10 times | 2 times
exercise: A £20 product has 10% off with a YouTuber's code. What does it cost? | £18 | £10 | £2 | £19
exercise: Why does the YouTuber want you to use her code? | She gets money each time someone uses it | She loses some of her own money every time you use it | The code makes the product better | The code donates money to your school
exercise: Sophie searches for trainers once and then sees trainer ads everywhere. Why? | Websites track what you look at and show matching ads | Trainers are the only thing that anyone is allowed to advertise online | Her phone is broken and keeps repeating | A friend is sending her the ads on purpose
exercise: Lucy's teacher asks what an ad wants you to feel. What did Lucy say? | Like I'm cool if I buy it | Like I should save my money | Like the product is a bit boring | Like I should ask a grown-up first
exercise: Cereal with a toy is £3.50 and plain cereal is £1.50. How much are you paying for the toy? | £2 | £5 | £3.50 | £1.50
exercise: Why do cereal ads for kids use cartoon animals and free toys? | They are a classic trick to make children want it | Cartoon cereals are healthier than other kinds of cereal | The law says all cereals must have cartoons | Free toys make the cereal cheaper to buy
exercise: A billboard says "The best pizza in town!" Who says so? | The pizza shop itself, as an opinion | A team of independent pizza judges after a tasting | The town council after a big taste test | Every single person who lives in the town
test: Freya screams, "I NEED it, it's bussin', everyone's got one!" ("Bussin'" means amazing.) Dad says, "That's the ad talking, babe." What does Dad mean? | The ad made her want it; she doesn't really need it | The TV is speaking to Freya by name | Freya will get the toy for free if she shouts loud enough | Everyone really has one, so she must buy it
test: Your mate goes, "If a famous footballer drinks it, it must make you play better." What's true? | He was paid to appear; it doesn't prove the drink helps | It's true, footballers only ever drink what helps them play | It's true, ads are checked to prove it works | Footballers are banned from drinking anything else
test: A Gen Z cousin says, "That ad's toy looks huge, but irl it's tiny, it's giving scam." ("Irl" means in real life; "it's giving" means it looks like.) What's happened? | The ad used camera tricks to make the toy look bigger | The toy shrank in the shop's cold air | The shop sold her a different, smaller toy | The ad used a giant child to make it look small
test: What would happen if Freya bought every toy she saw in ads with her £5 a week? | Her money would run out fast and she'd have little left | She would always have plenty of money left over | The adverts would pay her back each week | Nothing, because toys from ads are free
test: Mo groans, "An ad every 3 minutes, this is doing my head in, bro." He plays for an hour. How many ads? | 20 | 10 | 3 | 60
test: An Irish da says, "Says who? Says the pizza shop, ha!" What lesson is he teaching? | "Best" in an ad is the seller's opinion, not a checked fact | Pizza shops are never allowed to advertise | Every pizza shop is the best in its own town | Billboards are written by the council
test: In a cartoon, a talking cereal box promises you'll fly if you eat it. What should a kid think? | Ads make big promises to sell; cereal can't make you fly | It's true, cereal gives you super powers | It's only true if you eat the whole box | It's true but only on weekends
test: Kian's sister says the YouTuber's code gives "10% off, she gets paid." The product is £30. What do you pay with the code? | £27 | £20 | £3 | £29
test: Sophie freaks out: "Is the internet spying on me?!" What's the honest answer? | Websites track what you look at, often with cookies, to target ads | No, ads appear completely at random | Yes, a real person at the company reads all of your messages every night | Only your teacher can see what you search
test: An ad says "9 out of 10 dentists recommend it." What's a smart question to ask? | How many dentists were asked, and who chose them? | What colour is the toothpaste tube? | Is the tenth dentist a real person? | Why don't dentists brush their teeth?
test: Your nan says, "Back in my day ads were on telly only." Today a kid sees ads on games, videos and websites. Why are there so many? | Free apps and sites earn money by selling your attention to advertisers | The government makes everyone watch ads by law | Ads are free to make, so everyone does them | Kids ask for more ads, so sites add them
exam: It is given that an article is priced at £20 and that a discount of ten per cent is applied. Determine the price payable. | £18 | £10 | £2 | £19
exam: Ascertain the number of advertisements displayed during a period of thirty minutes, one advertisement being displayed every three minutes. | 10 | 3 | 33 | 90
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an endorsement? | The promotion of a product by a well-known person, frequently in exchange for payment | An independent scientific test of the quality of a product | A statutory limit upon the advertising of unhealthy food | A refund made in respect of an unsatisfactory product
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with sound reasoning upon encountering the claim "The best pizza in town". | Recognition of the claim as an opinion of the seller, not an established fact | Unqualified acceptance of the claim as an established fact | Immediate purchase of the product in order that the claim may be verified by tasting | Notification of the police in respect of the claim
